    await setDoc(doc(collection(db, "courses"), "MH4512"), {
    "code": "MH4512",
    "name": "CLINICAL TRIALS",
    "description": "Clinical trials are used to evaluate the safety, efficacy and feasibility of a treatment or a drug in human. It is a critical step in commercialising any pharmaceutical products. This course focuses on the statistical aspects of conducting clinical trials. We will introduce drug development process, best practices and regulatory requirements in the industry. Various trial designs and analysis methods at different phases of drug development will be discussed. This course is essential for students who plan to work with clinical or medical data. You will acquire knowledge in analysing clinical data and in interpreting the analysis results. You will also learn to appreciate medical research papers. Course Content: General Introduction to Clinical Trials: 1.\tDefinition of clinical trials 2.\tRegulatory requirements and guidelines 3.\tGood clinical practice 4.\tDrug development process 5.\tPhases of clinical trials Mixed Effects Model: 1.\tFixed and random effects 2.\tCrossover design, period and sequence effects of a trial 3.\tRepeated measures and effects of missing data 4.\tTotal, Inter- and intra-subject variances 5.\tUsing SAS?s Proc Mixed procedure 6.\tVariance and covariance structures Basic Pharmacokinetics: 1.\tPharmacokinetic and Pharmacodynamic 2.\tAbsorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Elimination of drugs 3.\tPharmacokinetic parameters and their estimation methods 4.\tLog-transformation, log-normal distribution and coefficient of variation Treatment Comparison in Various Designs: 1.\tBioequivalence, Superiority, and non-inferiority trials 2.\tTwo one-sided tests 3.\tEquivalence and non-inferiority margins 4.\tDose proportionality study, confidence interval approach 5.\tFood effects, hepatic or renal impairment, age or Gender comparisons 6.\tDrug-Drug interaction trials Nonparametric Methods: 1.\tSign test for median 2.\tWilcoxon signed-rank test 3.\tWilcoxon rank sum test 4.\tConfidence interval for median and difference of medians Longitudinal Data Analysis: 1.\tCharacteristics of Longitudinal Data 2.\tChange from baseline analysis 3.\tAnalysis of covariance approach 4.\tMixed effects approach with various covariance structures Sample Size and Power Calculations: 1.\tClinically meaningful difference and primary study endpoint 2.\tSample size estimation and power calculation for bioequivalence, superiority and non-inferiority trials",
    "prereq": "MH3500 & MH3510",
    "au_count": 4,
    "letter_grade": false,
    "indexes": [
        "70666"
    ]
});
